`@powerhousedao/builder-tools` is the toolkit behind the Powerhouse builder experience, and it is deliberately consumed through **subpath imports** rather than one barrel:

```ts
import { … } from "@powerhousedao/builder-tools/connect-studio";
import { … } from "@powerhousedao/builder-tools/connect-build";
import { … } from "@powerhousedao/builder-tools/document-model-editor";
import { … } from "@powerhousedao/builder-tools/editor-utils";
import "@powerhousedao/builder-tools/style.css";
```

The five modules map to five jobs: **Connect Studio** (managing and configuring connections), **Connect Build** (build utilities), the **Document Model Editor**, **Editor Utils** (common editor functionality), and **Connect Utils** (shared connection utilities).

Why the split is worth noticing rather than skimming: `connect-build` is build-time tooling and `document-model-editor` is a UI. A single entry point would drag one into a bundle that only wanted the other — so the subpaths are what let a runtime consumer take the editor pieces without the build machinery. The stylesheet is a separate import for the same reason.

This is also the package Vetra Studio's editing surface is assembled from, which is why the document-model editor lives here rather than in the design system.

